# Response headers for the static side of the app.
#
# This covers documents and assets only. The platform does NOT apply this file
# to responses produced by the Worker — those headers come from its own code,
# which for /api/* is hono/secure-headers in backend/src/app.ts. So the two
# halves of the origin are hardened in two places by necessity, not by choice;
# if you change a policy here, check whether app.ts needs the same change.
#
# That split survived the 2026-09-18 move from Pages to Workers unchanged:
# Workers static assets reads _headers with the same matching rules and the
# same carve-out for Worker-generated responses. Its sibling _redirects is NOT
# supported and was deleted in that move — the SPA fallback it described is now
# `not_found_handling` in the root wrangler.toml.

/*
  # The HTML document was the one thing with no frame protection. app.ts sends
  # X-Frame-Options on API responses, but that does nothing for the page a
  # browser actually renders, so until this file existed Compass could be
  # framed and clickjacked. Nothing legitimately embeds this app.
  #
  # This is the legacy header rather than a CSP `frame-ancestors`. A real CSP
  # is worth doing, and is a smaller job than it was when this file was
  # written: the document loaded Google Fonts then, and the legal round has
  # since self-hosted both families into /fonts, so there is no third-party
  # origin left for a policy to have to enumerate. What remains is the inline
  # theme script that prevents the dark-mode flash — a policy written
  # carelessly here still breaks first paint — and an inline script is exactly
  # the case a hash or a nonce covers. Still deferred, but on one complication
  # now rather than two.
  X-Frame-Options: DENY
  # Stops a browser from second-guessing Content-Type — the defense against an
  # asset being coaxed into executing as script.
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  # The modern browser default, stated rather than assumed: full URL to our own
  # origin, bare origin to anyone else. Nothing exercises it today — self-hosting
  # the fonts removed the last cross-origin request the app made, so there is no
  # Referer going anywhere but here. It is set anyway, because a default is
  # something browsers get to change, and because whatever third-party request
  # someone adds later should arrive under a policy that was already decided.
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin

/shared/*
  # The share link has no account behind it — the URL *is* the credential, and
  # the token sits in the path. Anything that leaks this URL leaks read access
  # to a student's plan until they revoke it.
  #
  # SharedPlanView already sets rel="noreferrer" on every outbound link it
  # renders, which is the leak that actually matters. This is the belt to that
  # pair of braces: it also covers what the page requests rather than links to,
  # and it does not depend on a future edit remembering the attribute.
  #
  # ORDERING IS LOAD-BEARING. Pages does not replace a header from an earlier
  # matching rule, it appends to it, so this page is actually served
  # `Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin, no-referrer`. That is
  # correct, and correct for a spec reason rather than by luck: a browser walks
  # the token list in order and keeps the last valid one, which is the
  # mechanism the spec provides for stating a fallback before a stronger
  # policy. Moving this block above the /* rule would silently invert it and
  # leave the share token's page on the weaker policy.
  Referrer-Policy: no-referrer
